    "question": "The diameter of a spherical planet is \\(6\\times10^4\\text{ km}\\).\n\n(a) Write down the radius of the planet. [1]\n\n(b) The volume of the planet can be expressed in the form \\(\\pi\\left(a\\times10^k\\right)\\text{ km}^3\\), where \\(1\\le a<10\\) and \\(k\\in\\mathbb{Z}\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(a\\) and the value of \\(k\\). [3]",
    "hint": "How are radius and diameter related, and what happens to the power of \\(10\\) when the radius is cubed in the sphere-volume formula?",
    "answer": "(a) The radius is half the diameter: \\(\\boxed{3\\times10^4\\text{ km}}\\), \\(\\boxed{30000\\text{ km}}\\), or \\(\\boxed{3\\mathbin{\\cdot}10^4\\text{ km}}\\). A1 [1 mark]\n\n(b) Substitute the radius into the volume formula for a sphere:\n\\[\n\\frac43\\pi(3\\times10^4)^3\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\frac43\\pi(30000)^3. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nCubing \\(3\\times10^4\\) gives \\(27\\times10^{12}\\), so\n\\[\n\\frac43\\pi\\times27\\times10^{12}\n=\\pi(36\\times10^{12})\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\frac43\\pi\\times27000000000000. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nWriting the coefficient in the required form gives\n\\[\n\\pi(3.6\\times10^{13})\\text{ km}^3,\n\\]\nso \\(\\boxed{a=3.6}\\) and \\(\\boxed{k=13}\\). A1 [3 marks]",
