    "question": "Let \\(a\\) be a constant, where \\(a>1\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(a^2+\\left(\\frac{a^2-1}{2}\\right)^2=\\left(\\frac{a^2+1}{2}\\right)^2\\). [3]\n\nConsider a right-angled triangle with sides of length \\(a\\), \\(\\left(\\frac{a^2-1}{2}\\right)\\), and \\(\\left(\\frac{a^2+1}{2}\\right)\\).\n\n(b) Find an expression for the area of the triangle in terms of \\(a\\). [2]",
    "hint": "For part (a), expand only the left-hand side; for part (b), identify the two perpendicular sides.",
    "answer": "(a)\n\n**Guide:** Work on one side of the identity only and simplify it until it matches the other side.\n\nExpand \\((a^2-1)^2\\) on the left-hand side. (M1)\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{LHS}\n&=a^2+\\frac{a^4-2a^2+1}{4}\\\\\n&=\\frac{4a^2+a^4-2a^2+1}{4}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\\\\\n&=\\frac{a^4+2a^2+1}{4}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{a^2+1}{2}\\right)^2\n=\\operatorname{RHS}. \\qquad \\text{AG}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, the left-hand side equals the right-hand side, so the identity is proved.\n\n> **Note:** Award at most M1A0A0 if both sides are manipulated simultaneously. Do not award the final A1 if later working contradicts the shown identity.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(b) The perpendicular sides have lengths \\(a\\) and \\(\\frac{a^2-1}{2}\\). (M1)\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\text{Area}\n=\\frac12a\\left(\\frac{a^2-1}{2}\\right)\n=\\boxed{\\frac{a(a^2-1)}{4}},\n\\]\nor equivalently \\(\\boxed{\\frac{a^3-a}{4}}\\). A1\n\n[2 marks]",
