    "question": "(a) The expression \\(\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}-5}{\\sqrt{x}}\\) can be written as \\(3-5x^p\\).\n\nWrite down the value of \\(p\\). [1]\n\n(b) Hence, find the value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^9\\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}-5}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)\\,dx\\). [4]",
    "hint": "Rewrite \\(1/\\sqrt{x}\\) as a power of \\(x\\), then integrate term by term and apply the limits.",
    "answer": "(a) Since\n\\[\n\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}-5}{\\sqrt{x}}=3-5x^{-1/2},\n\\]\n\\(\\boxed{p=-\\frac12}\\). A1 [1 mark]\n\n(b) Integrating term by term gives\n\\[\n\\int\\left(3-5x^{-1/2}\\right)\\,dx\n=3x-10x^{1/2}+C. \\qquad \\text{A1A1}\n\\]\nSubstitute the limits and subtract: (M1)\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3x-10\\sqrt{x}\\right]_1^9\n&=3(9)-10\\sqrt9-\\left(3(1)-10\\sqrt1\\right)\\\\\n&=27-30-(3-10)\\\\\n&=\\boxed{4}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** For a correct final answer with no working, apply the source markscheme allocation A1A0A0A1.\n\n[4 marks]",
