    "question": "(a) Show that \\(2x-3-\\frac{6}{x-1}=\\frac{2x^2-5x-3}{x-1}\\), \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x\\ne 1\\). [2]\n\n(b) Hence or otherwise, solve the equation \\(2\\sin 2\\theta-3-\\frac{6}{\\sin 2\\theta-1}=0\\) for \\(0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi\\), \\(\\theta\\ne\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\). [5]",
    "hint": "How can you put the terms in part (a) over a common denominator, and what substitution turns part (b) into the same algebraic form?",
    "answer": "(a)\n\n**Guide:** Put every term over the common denominator \\(x-1\\), then simplify the numerator.\n\nUsing the common denominator \\(x-1\\), (M1)\n\nUsing expansion before combining gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x-3-\\frac{6}{x-1}\n&=\\frac{2x(x-1)-3(x-1)-6}{x-1}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2x^2-2x-3x+3-6}{x-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAlternatively, the same common-denominator calculation can be written as\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x-3-\\frac{6}{x-1}\n&=\\frac{(2x-3)(x-1)}{x-1}-\\frac{6}{x-1}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2x^2-5x+3}{x-1}-\\frac{6}{x-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBoth equivalent expansions produce the numerator \\(2x^2-5x-3\\). A1\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n2x-3-\\frac{6}{x-1}\n=\\frac{2x^2-5x-3}{x-1}. \\qquad \\text{AG}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, the stated identity is true for \\(x\\ne1\\).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b)\n\n**Guide:** Apply the identity from part (a) with \\(x=\\sin 2\\theta\\), then solve the resulting quadratic in \\(\\sin 2\\theta\\).\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2\\sin^2 2\\theta-5\\sin 2\\theta-3}{\\sin 2\\theta-1}=0. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nBecause the denominator is non-zero on the stated domain, the numerator must be zero:\n\\[\n2\\sin^2 2\\theta-5\\sin 2\\theta-3=0.\n\\]\n\nFactorise in the form \\((2\\sin 2\\theta+a)(\\sin 2\\theta+b)\\). (M1)\n\\[\n(2\\sin 2\\theta+1)(\\sin 2\\theta-3)=0.\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** Accept any temporary variable in place of \\(\\sin 2\\theta\\) during the factorisation.\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2\\theta=-\\frac12\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\sin 2\\theta=3. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nOnly \\(\\sin 2\\theta=-\\frac12\\) is possible because a sine value must lie between \\(-1\\) and \\(1\\).\n\nFor \\(0\\le 2\\theta\\le 2\\pi\\),\n\\[\n2\\theta=\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n2\\theta=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\theta=\\frac{7\\pi}{12},\\ \\frac{11\\pi}{12}}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** Award the intermediate A1 only for the feasible value \\(\\sin 2\\theta=-\\frac12\\). Final answers must be in radians; award the final A0 if additional answers are given.\n\n[5 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §12.3 Trigonometric identities and equations · p. 517",
    "teacherNote": ""
