    "question": "Consider the function \\(f(x)=a^x\\), where \\(x,a\\in\\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x>0\\), and \\(a>1\\).\n\nThe graph of \\(f\\) contains the point \\(\\left(\\frac23,4\\right)\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(a=8\\). [2]\n\n(b) Write down an expression for \\(f^{-1}(x)\\). [1]\n\n(c) Find the value of \\(f^{-1}(\\sqrt{32})\\). [3]\n\nConsider the arithmetic sequence \\(\\log_8 27,\\ \\log_8 p,\\ \\log_8 q,\\ \\log_8 125\\), where \\(p>1\\) and \\(q>1\\).\n\n(d.i) Show that \\(27\\), \\(p\\), \\(q\\), and \\(125\\) are four consecutive terms in a geometric sequence. [4]\n\n(d.ii) Find the value of \\(p\\) and the value of \\(q\\). [5]",
    "hint": "What does the given point tell you about the base \\(a\\), and how do equal differences between logarithms become equal ratios between their arguments?",
    "answer": "(a) Since the graph contains \\(\\left(\\frac23,4\\right)\\),\n\\[\nf\\left(\\frac23\\right)=4\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\na^{2/3}=4. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nUndoing the exponent gives any of the equivalent steps\n\\[\na=4^{3/2},\n\\quad\na=(2^2)^{3/2},\n\\quad\na^2=64,\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\sqrt[3]{a}=2. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\boxed{a=8}. \\qquad \\text{AG}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, \\(a=8\\), as required.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) \\(\\boxed{f^{-1}(x)=\\log_8 x}\\), \\(\\boxed{f^{-1}(x)=\\log_a x}\\), or \\(\\boxed{f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln 8}}\\). A1 [1 mark]\n\n(c) A correct substitution into the inverse function is\n\\[\n\\log_8\\sqrt{32}\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n8^x=32^{1/2}. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nUsing logarithm or index laws then gives any equivalent step such as\n\\[\n\\frac12\\log_8 32,\n\\quad\n\\frac52\\log_8 2,\n\\quad\n\\log_8 2=\\frac13,\n\\quad\n\\log_2 2^{5/2},\n\\quad\n\\log_2 8=3,\n\\quad\n\\frac{\\ln 2^{5/2}}{\\ln 2^3},\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n2^{3x}=2^{5/2}. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{f^{-1}(\\sqrt{32})=\\frac56}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(d.i)\n\n**Guide:** Use equal consecutive differences in the logarithmic arithmetic sequence, then convert the resulting logarithmic equalities into ratios.\n\nEqual differences in the arithmetic sequence may be written as (M1)\n\\[\nu_2-u_1=u_4-u_3\\;(=u_3-u_2).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\log_8 p-\\log_8 27=\\log_8 125-\\log_8 q\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log_8 125-\\log_8 q=\\log_8 q-\\log_8 p.\n\\]\nUsing the quotient law,\n\\[\n\\log_8\\left(\\frac{p}{27}\\right)\n=\\log_8\\left(\\frac{125}{q}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\log_8\\left(\\frac{125}{q}\\right)\n=\\log_8\\left(\\frac{q}{p}\\right). \\qquad \\text{A1A1}\n\\]\nSince equal logarithms with the same base have equal arguments,\n\\[\n\\frac{p}{27}=\\frac{125}{q}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\frac{125}{q}=\\frac{q}{p}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\nThese are three equal consecutive ratios. AG\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, \\(27\\), \\(p\\), \\(q\\), and \\(125\\) are four consecutive terms of a geometric sequence.\n\n> **Note:** If the sequence is merely assumed to be geometric, award no marks for part (d.i). If \\(r=\\frac53\\) is found here, credit it in part (d.ii).\n\n[4 marks]\n\n(d.ii) Use the geometric-sequence relation\n\\[\nu_4=u_1r^3\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n125=27r^3. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\nr=\\frac53. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\np=27r\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\frac{125}{q}=\\frac53. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nSo\n\\[\n\\boxed{p=45,\\ q=75}. \\qquad \\text{A1A1}\n\\]\n\n[5 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a) §9.1 Exponents · p. 396\n(b)–(d.i) §9.2 Logarithms · p. 407\n(d.ii) §1.2 Arithmetic and geometric sequences · p. 13",
    "teacherNote": ""
