    "question": "Consider two consecutive positive integers, \\(n\\) and \\(n+1\\).\n\nShow that the difference of their squares is equal to the sum of the two integers. [4]",
    "hint": "Expand \\((n+1)^2-n^2\\), then compare the result with \\(n+(n+1)\\).",
    "answer": "**Guide:** Subtract the squares in the correct order, then compare the simplified result with the sum of the two integers.\n\nSubtract the square of the smaller integer from the square of the larger integer: (M1)\n\\[\n(n+1)^2-n^2.\n\\]\nExpanding in the correct order gives\n\\[\n(n+1)^2-n^2=n^2+2n+1-n^2=2n+1. \\qquad \\text{A1A1}\n\\]\nBut\n\\[\nn+(n+1)=2n+1. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, the difference of the squares is equal to the sum of the two integers. \\(\\text{AG}\\)\n\n> **Note:** Do not award the final A1 unless all preceding working is correct. If the expansion and subtraction order are correct, award full marks when the equivalent sum \\(n+1+n\\) is used.\n\n[4 marks]",
