    "question": "In the expansion of \\((x+k)^7\\), where \\(k\\in\\mathbb{R}\\), the coefficient of the term in \\(x^5\\) is \\(63\\).\n\nFind the possible values of \\(k\\). [5]",
    "hint": "Which binomial term contains \\(x^5k^2\\), and what must its coefficient equal?",
    "answer": "**Step 1: Identify the required binomial term**\n\nApply the binomial expansion to \\((x+k)^7\\). (M1)\nThe term containing \\(x^5\\) is\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{2}x^5k^2. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{2}=21, \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Apply the coefficient condition**\n\nThe coefficient condition gives\n\\[\n21k^2=63,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nk^2=3. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=\\pm\\sqrt{3}}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** If working is shown but only \\(k=\\sqrt3\\) is retained, apply the source markscheme’s final A0 restriction.\n\n[5 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §1.5 The binomial theorem · p. 44",
    "teacherNote": ""
