    "isGraphQuestion": false,
    "graphType": "none",
    "image": "images/QB000044_question_01_source.png",
    "imageAlt": "Table of values for the one-to-one functions f and g",
    "question": "The following table shows values of \\(f(x)\\) and \\(g(x)\\) for different values of \\(x\\).\n\nBoth \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) are one-to-one functions.\n\n![Table of values for the one-to-one functions f and g](images/QB000044_question_01_source.png)\n\n(a) Find \\(g(0)\\). [1]\n\n(b) Find \\((f\\circ g)(0)\\). [2]\n\n(c) Find the value of \\(x\\) such that \\(f(x)=0\\). [2]",
    "hint": "Read the correct row and column first, then use the output of \\(g(0)\\) as the input of \\(f\\).",
    "answer": "(a) From the table,\n\\[\n\\boxed{g(0)=-2}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(b) Use the composite function in the stated order: (M1)\n\\[\nf(g(0))=f(-2).\n\\]\nFrom the table,\n\\[\n\\boxed{(f\\circ g)(0)=8}. \\qquad \\text{A1}\n\\]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c) The table shows that \\(f(3)=0\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=3}. \\qquad \\text{A2}\n\\]\n\n[2 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a), (c) §2.2 Functional notation · p. 69\n(b) §2.5 Composite functions · p. 85",
