    "imageAlt": "Paired average weekly practice times and diploma scores",
    "question": "In Lucy’s music academy, eight students took their piano diploma examination and achieved scores out of 150. For her records, Lucy decided to record the average number of hours per week each student reported practising in the weeks prior to their examination. These results are summarized in the table below.\n\n![Paired average weekly practice times and diploma scores](images/QB000160_question_01_source.png)\n\n(a) Find Pearson’s product-moment correlation coefficient, \\(r\\), for these data. [2]\n\n(b) The relationship between the variables can be modelled by the regression equation \\(D=ah+b\\).\n\nWrite down the value of \\(a\\) and the value of \\(b\\). [1]\n\n(c) One of these eight students was disappointed with her result and wished she had practised more. Based on the given data, determine how her score could have been expected to alter had she practised an extra five hours per week. [2]",
    "hint": "What does the regression slope measure in this setting? How does a change in practice time affect the predicted score?",
    "answer": "(a) Calculate the correlation from the paired data. (M1)\n\n\\(\\boxed{r=0.884}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.883529\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n> **Note:** The method mark may also be awarded for a correct regression coefficient or \\(r^2=0.780624\\ldots\\), seen in (a) or (b).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) The regression coefficients are \\(\\boxed{a=1.37,\\ b=64.5}\\) (unrounded: \\(1.36609\\ldots,64.5171\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(c) Multiply the slope by the additional five hours. (M1)\n\nThe increase is \\(5(1.36609\\ldots)=6.83045\\ldots\\), so approximately \\(\\boxed{7}\\) marks. A1\n\n> **Note:** Accept \\(6\\), \\(6.83\\), or \\(6.85\\).\n\n[2 marks]",
