    "isGraphQuestion": false,
    "graphType": "none",
    "image": "images/QB000157_question_01_source.png",
    "imageAlt": "Probability table for values 0, 1, 2, and 3 with probabilities q, 4p squared, p, and 0.7 minus 4p squared",
    "question": "A discrete random variable \\(X\\) has the following probability distribution.\n\n![Probability table for values 0, 1, 2, and 3 with probabilities q, 4p squared, p, and 0.7 minus 4p squared](images/QB000157_question_01_source.png)\n\n(a) Find an expression for \\(q\\) in terms of \\(p\\). [2]\n\n(b.i) Find the value of \\(p\\) which gives the largest value of \\(E(X)\\). [3]\n\n(b.ii) Hence, find the largest value of \\(E(X)\\). [1]",
    "hint": "What must the probabilities sum to? How does the expected value depend on the unknown parameter?",
    "answer": "(a) Since the probabilities sum to one,\n\\[\nq+4p^2+p+0.7-4p^2=1. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{q=0.3-p}\\). A1 N2\n\n> **Note:** This question is from an exam for a previous syllabus and may contain minor differences in marking or structure.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b.i)\n\n**Guide:** Express the expected value as a function of the parameter, then locate the maximum of that function.\n\n\\[\nE(X)=0(0.3-p)+1(4p^2)+2p+3(0.7-4p^2). \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nUse the TI-84 to maximize \\(-8p^2+2p+2.1\\). (M1)\n\nThe maximizing value is \\(\\boxed{p=\\frac18}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{p=0.125}\\), exactly. A1 N3\n\n> **Note:** Accept \\(x=\\frac18\\).\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(b.ii) The maximum expected value is \\(\\boxed{2.225}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{\\frac{89}{40}}\\), or \\(\\boxed{2.23}\\) to three significant figures. A1 N1\n\n[1 mark]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(b.ii) §14.1 Random variables · p. 584\n(b.i) §3.4 Graphing quadratic functions · p. 142",
    "teacherNote": ""
