    "question": "A factory manufactures lamps. It is known that the probability that a lamp is found to be defective is \\(0.05\\). A random sample of \\(30\\) lamps is tested.\n\n(a) Find the probability that there is at least one defective lamp in the sample. [3]\n\n(b) Given that there is at least one defective lamp in the sample, find the probability that there are at most two defective lamps. [4]",
    "hint": "Which event is the complement of at least one defective lamp? Once the box is known to contain a defect, which outcomes remain in the sample space?",
    "answer": "(a) Let \\(X\\sim B(30,0.05)\\). Use the complement of no defects. (M1)\n\\[\nP(X\\ge1)=1-0.95^{30}. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{P(X\\ge1)=0.785}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.785361\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n> **Note:** The two method marks are independent.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(b) Restrict the sample space to \\(X\\ge1\\). (M1)\n\\[\nP(X\\le2\\mid X\\ge1)=\\frac{P(X=1)+P(X=2)}{P(X\\ge1)}\n=\\frac{0.597540\\ldots}{0.785361\\ldots}. \\qquad \\text{(A1)(A1)}\n\\]\nThe probability is \\(\\boxed{0.761}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.760847\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n> **Note:** Recognition of conditional probability must be shown in this question's context, not solely as a general formula.\n\n[4 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a) §14.2 The binomial distribution · p. 592\n(b) §8.3 Independent and dependent events and conditional probability · p. 375",
    "teacherNote": ""
