    "question": "At a school, \\(70\\%\\) of the students play a sport and \\(20\\%\\) of the students are involved in theatre. \\(18\\%\\) of the students do neither activity.\n\nA student is selected at random.\n\n(a) Find the probability that the student plays a sport and is involved in theatre. [2]\n\n(b) Find the probability that the student is involved in theatre, but does not play a sport. [2]\n\nAt the school \\(48\\%\\) of the students are girls, and \\(25\\%\\) of the girls are involved in theatre.\n\nA student is selected at random. Let \\(G\\) be the event “the student is a girl” and let \\(T\\) be the event “the student is involved in theatre”.\n\n(c) Find \\(P(G\\cap T)\\). [2]\n\n(d) Determine if the events \\(G\\) and \\(T\\) are independent. Justify your answer. [2]",
    "hint": "How does the probability of neither activity determine the union? Which joint probability is needed to test independence?",
    "answer": "(a) Relate the two event probabilities, the probability of neither, and the overlap. (M1)\n\\[\n0.7+0.2+0.18-P(S\\cap T)=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{P(S\\cap T)=0.08}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{8\\%}\\). A1\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) Subtract the overlap from the theatre probability. (M1)\n\n\\(\\boxed{P(T\\cap S')=0.2-0.08=0.12}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{12\\%}\\). A1\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c) Multiply the probability of being a girl by the conditional theatre probability. (M1)\n\\(\\boxed{P(G\\cap T)=0.48(0.25)=0.12}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{12\\%}\\). A1\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(d) The product is \\(P(G)P(T)=0.48(0.2)=0.096\\). A1\n\nSince \\(0.096\\ne0.12=P(G\\cap T)\\), the events are not independent. R1\n\n> **Note:** Do not award A0R1.\n\n[2 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(b) §8.2 Representing probabilities: Venn diagrams and sample spaces · p. 363\n(c)–(d) §8.3 Independent and dependent events and conditional probability · p. 375",
    "teacherNote": ""
