    "question": "A random sample of nine adults were selected to see whether sleeping well affected their reaction times to a visual stimulus. Each adult’s reaction time was measured twice.\n\nThe first measurement for reaction time was taken on a morning after the adult had slept well. The second measurement was taken on a morning after the same adult had not slept well.\n\nThe box and whisker diagrams for the reaction times, measured in seconds, are shown below.\n\n![Two horizontal box plots comparing reaction times after sleeping well and not sleeping well](images/QB000175_question_01_source.png)\n\nConsider the box and whisker diagram representing the reaction times after sleeping well.\n\n(a) State the median reaction time after sleeping well. [1]\n\n(b) Verify that the measurement of \\(0.46\\) seconds is not an outlier. [3]\n\n(c) State why it appears that the mean reaction time is greater than the median reaction time. [1]\n\n(d) Now consider the two box and whisker diagrams.\n\nComment on whether these box and whisker diagrams provide any evidence that might suggest that not sleeping well causes an increase in reaction time. [1]",
    "hint": "What do the box plot and quartiles tell you about the upper outlier fence and skewness? Which comparison of the two groups supports a conclusion about sleep?",
    "answer": "(a) The median is \\(\\boxed{0.28\\text{ s}}\\). A1\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(b) \\(\\mathrm{IQR}=0.35-0.27=0.08\\). (A1)\n\nThe upper fence is \\(0.35+1.5(0.08)=0.47\\). (A1)\n\nThe maximum, \\(0.46\\), is below \\(0.47\\). R1\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, the longest recorded reaction time is not an outlier. AG\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(c) The median is closer to the lower quartile, indicating positive skew. R1\n\n> **Note:** Any correct explanation is accepted, including a clearly labelled diagram. An additional incorrect statement invalidates the reasoning mark.\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(d) The reaction times after not sleeping well are generally higher than after sleeping well, supporting the claim that insufficient sleep slows reactions. R1\n\n> **Note:** Any relevant correct statement about the medians or quartiles is accepted. A comparison of means is not accepted from these box plots. An additional incorrect statement invalidates R1, even if another statement is correct. “Correlation does not imply causation” alone earns R0.\n\n[1 mark]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(d) §6.4 Measures of dispersion · p. 296",
    "teacherNote": ""
