    "isGraphQuestion": true,
    "graphType": "graph",
    "image": "images/QB000155_question_01_source.png",
    "imageAlt": "Three-set Venn diagram for R, W, and V, with numerical regions and unknowns a and b",
    "question": "On a school excursion, \\(100\\) students visited an amusement park. The amusement park’s main attractions are rollercoasters (\\(R\\)), water slides (\\(W\\)), and virtual reality rides (\\(V\\)).\n\nThe students were asked which main attractions they visited. The results are shown in the Venn diagram.\n\n![Three-set Venn diagram for R, W, and V, with numerical regions and unknowns a and b](images/QB000155_question_01_source.png)\n\nA total of \\(74\\) students visited the rollercoasters or the water slides.\n\n(a.i) Find the value of \\(a\\). [2]\n\n(a.ii) Find the value of \\(b\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find the number of students who visited at least two types of main attraction. [2]\n\n(c) Write down the value of \\(n( R\\cap W)\\). [1]\n\n(d.i) Find the probability that a randomly selected student visited the rollercoasters. [2]\n\n(d.ii) Find the probability that a randomly selected student visited the virtual reality rides. [1]\n\n(e) Hence determine whether the events in parts (d)(i) and (d)(ii) are independent. Justify your reasoning. [2]",
    "hint": "Which regions belong to each union or intersection? What probability equality would establish independence?",
    "answer": "(a.i) Subtract the known regions of \\(R\\cup W\\) from its total:\n\\[\na=74-(32+12+10+9+5)=\\boxed{6}. \\qquad \\text{(M1)(A1)(G2)}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for setting up a correct expression.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(a.ii) Count all visitors, including those outside the three sets:\n\\[\nb=100-(74+18)=\\boxed{8}. \\qquad \\text{(M1)(A1)(ft)(G2)}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for a correct expression, following through from (a.i) only when \\(a\\ge0\\). Award the final A1(ft) only when \\(b\\ge0\\).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) Add the regions belonging to at least two sets:\n\\[\n9+5+12+10=\\boxed{36}. \\qquad \\text{(M1)(A1)(G2)}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for adding \\(9,5,12,10\\).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c) \\(\\boxed{n(R\\cap W)=14}\\). (A1)\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(d.i) \\(P(R)=\\boxed{\\frac{58}{100}}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{\\frac{29}{50}}\\), \\(\\boxed{0.58}\\), or \\(\\boxed{58\\%}\\). (A1)(A1)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award A1 for the correct numerator and A1 for the correct denominator. Award A0 for \\(58\\) alone.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(d.ii) \\(P(V)=\\boxed{\\frac{45}{100}}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{\\frac9{20}}\\), \\(\\boxed{0.45}\\), or \\(\\boxed{45\\%}\\). (A1)(ft)\n\n> **Note:** Follow through from the denominator in (d.i).\n\n[1 mark]\n\n(e) The events are not independent. (A1)(ft)\n\\[\nP(R)P(V)=0.58(0.45)=0.261\\ne0.17=P(R\\cap V). \\qquad \\text{(R1)}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** A numerical comparison is required; do not award A1R0. Follow through from (d.i) and (d.ii).\n\n[2 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a.i)–(d.ii) §8.2 Representing probabilities: Venn diagrams and sample spaces · p. 363\n(e) §8.3 Independent and dependent events and conditional probability · p. 375",
    "teacherNote": ""
