    "image": "images/QB000158_question_01_source.png",
    "imageAlt": "Weekend shooting records for Emlyn and Johan",
    "question": "Emlyn plays many games of basketball for his school team. The number of minutes he plays in each game follows a normal distribution with mean \\(m\\) minutes.\n\nIn any game there is a \\(30 \\%\\) chance he will play less than \\(13.6 minutes\\).\n\n(a) Sketch a diagram to represent this information. [2]\n\nIn any game there is a \\(70 \\%\\) chance he will play less than \\(17.8 minutes\\).\n\n(b) Show that \\(m=15.7\\). [2]\n\nThe standard deviation of the number of minutes Emlyn plays in any game is \\(4\\).\n\n(c.i) Find the probability that Emlyn plays between \\(13 minutes\\) and \\(18 minutes\\) in a game. [2]\n\n(c.ii) Find the probability that Emlyn plays more than \\(20 minutes\\) in a game. [2]\n\nThere is a \\(60 \\%\\) chance Emlyn plays less than \\(x\\) minutes in a game.\n\n(d) Find the value of \\(x\\). [2]\n\nEmlyn will play in two basketball games today.\n\n(e) Find the probability he plays between \\(13 minutes\\) and \\(18 minutes\\) in one game and more than \\(20 minutes\\) in the other game. [3]\n\nEmlyn and his teammate Johan each practise shooting the basketball multiple times from a point \\(X\\). A record of their performance over the weekend is shown in the table below.\n\n![Weekend shooting records for Emlyn and Johan](images/QB000158_question_01_source.png)\n\nOn Monday, Emlyn and Johan will practise and each will shoot \\(200\\) times from point \\(X\\).\n\n(f) Find the expected number of successful shots Emlyn will make on Monday, based on the results from Saturday and Sunday. [2]\n\n(g) Emlyn claims the results from Saturday and Sunday show that his expected number of successful shots will be more than Johan’s.\n\nDetermine if Emlyn’s claim is correct. Justify your reasoning. [2]",
    "hint": "How does symmetry connect the two given normal probabilities? For the two games, in how many orders can the required events occur?",
    "answer": "(a)\n\n![Normal curve with mean m and the left tail below 13.6 shaded](images/QB000158_answer_01_source.png)\n\nThe bell-shaped curve has its mean labelled \\(m\\), with the left-hand region below \\(13.6\\) shaded. (A1)(A1)\n\n> **Note:** This question is from an exam for a previous syllabus and may contain minor differences in marking or structure. Award A1 for a bell-shaped curve with \\(m\\) or \\(13.6\\) indicated and A1 for approximately correct shading.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) Since \\(P(T<17.8)=0.7\\), \\(P(T>17.8)=0.3\\). (M1)\n\nEqual tail probabilities place the two boundary values equally far from the mean, so\n\\[\nm=\\frac{13.6+17.8}{2}. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:** Therefore, \\(m=15.7\\), as required. (AG)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for the correct probability equation. Strict or weak inequalities are accepted in (b), (c), and (d). An unsupported midpoint calculation earns M0M1. Award at most M1M0 if the final value is absent. Using the given answer \\(m=15.7\\) and later value \\(\\sigma=4\\) merely to check a probability earns M0M0.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c.i) Calculate \\(P(13\\le T\\le18)\\) for \\(T\\sim N(15.7,4^2)\\). (M1)\n\nThe probability is \\(\\boxed{0.468}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{46.8\\%}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.467516\\ldots\\)). (A1)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for the correct probability equation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c.ii) Calculate \\(P(T\\ge20)\\). (M1)\n\nThe probability is \\(\\boxed{0.141}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{14.1\\%}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.141187\\ldots\\)). (A1)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for the correct probability equation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(d) Solve \\(P(T<x)=0.6\\) using inverse normal. (M1)\n\nThis gives \\(\\boxed{x=16.7}\\) minutes (unrounded: \\(16.7133\\ldots\\)). (A1)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for the correct probability equation.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(e) Either game can be the one in the shorter interval:\n\\[\n2(0.467516\\ldots)(0.141187\\ldots). \\qquad \\text{(M1)(M1)}\n\\]\nThe probability is \\(\\boxed{0.132}\\), equivalently \\(\\boxed{13.2\\%}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.132014\\ldots\\)). (A1)(ft)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for multiplying the probabilities from (c.i) and (c.ii), and M1 for multiplying the product by two. Follow through from (c). An unsupported \\(0.066007\\ldots\\) earns G0.\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(f) Combine the successful shots and attempts from both days:\n\\[\n\\frac{69}{102}(200)=135.294\\ldots. \\qquad \\text{(M1)}\n\\]\nThe expected number is \\(\\boxed{135}\\). (A1)(G2)\n\n> **Note:** Award M1 for the correct probability multiplied by \\(200\\).\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(g) Johan's expectation is \\(\\frac{67}{98}(200)=136.734\\ldots\\). (A1)\n\nEmlyn's claim is incorrect because \\(135<137\\), or, before rounding, \\(135.294\\ldots<136.734\\ldots\\). (R1)\n\n> **Note:** The source's qualification awards M1 for seeing \\(137\\) or \\(136.734\\ldots\\). Both conclusion and comparison are required for R1. Award at most A0R1(ft) for consistent incorrect methods in (f) and (g).\n\n[2 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(d) §14.3 The normal distribution · p. 603\n(e) §8.3 Independent and dependent events and conditional probability · p. 375\n(f)–(g) §8.1 Theoretical and experimental probability · p. 354",
    "teacherNote": ""
