    "question": "The flight times, \\(T\\) minutes, between two cities can be modelled by a normal distribution with a mean of \\(75\\) minutes and a standard deviation of \\(σ\\) minutes.\n\n(a) Given that \\(2\\%\\) of the flight times are longer than \\(82\\) minutes, find the value of \\(σ\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the probability that a randomly selected flight will have a flight time of more than \\(80\\) minutes. [2]\n\n(c) Given that a flight between the two cities takes longer than \\(80\\) minutes, find the probability that it takes less than \\(82\\) minutes. [4]\n\nOn a particular day, there are \\(64\\) flights scheduled between these two cities.\n\n(d) Find the expected number of flights that will have a flight time of more than \\(80\\) minutes. [3]\n\n(e) Find the probability that more than \\(6\\) of the flights on this particular day will have a flight time of more than \\(80\\) minutes. [3]",
    "hint": "How can a known tail probability determine the normal standard deviation? How does a single-flight probability become a model for the number of flights?",
    "answer": "(a) Use inverse normal with cumulative probability \\(0.98\\). (M1)\n\\[\n\\frac{82-75}{\\sigma}=2.0537\\ldots. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nHence \\(\\boxed{\\sigma=3.41}\\) (unrounded: \\(3.4084\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(b) Calculate \\(P(T>80)\\) with the normal model. (M1)\n\n\\(\\boxed{P(T>80)=0.0712}\\) (unrounded: \\(0.071193\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(c) Apply conditional probability within the flights lasting longer than \\(80\\) minutes. (M1)\n\nFind the probability of the intersection, \\(P(80<T<82)\\). (M1)\n\\[\nP(T<82\\mid T>80)=\\frac{0.071193\\ldots-0.02}{0.071193\\ldots}. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nThe probability is \\(\\boxed{0.719}\\). A1\n\n[4 marks]\n\n(d) Model the number as \\(X\\sim B(64,0.071193\\ldots)\\). (M1)\n\\[\nE(X)=64(0.071193\\ldots). \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{E(X)=4.56}\\) (unrounded: \\(4.55635\\ldots\\)). A1\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(e) Use the complementary cumulative binomial probability. (M1)\n\\[\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le6)=1-0.83088\\ldots. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\boxed{P(X>6)=0.169}\\). A1\n\n[3 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(b) §14.3 The normal distribution · p. 603\n(c) §8.3 Independent and dependent events and conditional probability · p. 375\n(d)–(e) §14.2 The binomial distribution · p. 592",
    "teacherNote": ""
