    "question": "Fiona walks from her house to a bus stop where she gets a bus to school. Her time, \\(W\\) minutes, to walk to the bus stop is normally distributed with \\(W∼N(12, {3}^{2})\\).\n\nFiona always leaves her house at 07:15. The first bus that she can get departs at 07:30.\n\n(a) Find the probability that it will take Fiona between \\(15\\) minutes and \\(30\\) minutes to walk to the bus stop. [2]\n\nThe length of time, \\(B\\) minutes, of the bus journey to Fiona’s school is normally distributed with \\(B∼N(50, {σ}^{2})\\). The probability that the bus journey takes less than \\(60\\) minutes is \\(0.941\\).\n\n(b) Find \\(σ\\). [3]\n\n(c) Find the probability that the bus journey takes less than \\(45\\) minutes. [2]\n\nIf Fiona misses the first bus, there is a second bus which departs at 07:45. She must arrive at school by 08:30 to be on time. Fiona will not arrive on time if she misses both buses. The variables \\(W\\) and \\(B\\) are independent.\n\n(d) Find the probability that Fiona will arrive on time. [5]\n\n(e) This year, Fiona will go to school on \\(183\\) days.\n\nCalculate the number of days Fiona is expected to arrive on time. [2]",
    "hint": "Which combinations of walking time and bus journey time get Fiona to school on time? How can those disjoint routes be combined?",
    "answer": "(a) For \\(W\\sim N(12,3^2)\\),\n\\[\nP(15<W<30)=0.158655\\ldots\\approx\\boxed{0.159}. \\qquad \\text{A2 N2}\n\\]\n\n> **Note:** This question is from an exam for a previous syllabus and may contain minor differences in marking or structure.\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(b) The standard-normal value corresponding to cumulative probability \\(0.941\\) is \\(z=1.56322\\ldots\\). (A1)\n\\[\n\\frac{60-50}{\\sigma}=1.56322\\ldots. \\qquad \\text{(A1)}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\boxed{\\sigma=6.40}\\) minutes (unrounded: \\(6.39703\\ldots\\)). A1 N3\n\n[3 marks]\n\n(c) \\(P(B<45)=0.217221\\ldots\\approx\\boxed{0.217}\\). A2 N2\n\n[2 marks]\n\n(d)\n\n**Guide:** Separate the journey into catching the first bus and catching the second bus; the time left for the bus journey differs between these routes.\n\nThe two successful routes are \\(W\\le15, B<60\\) and \\(15<W\\le30, B<45\\). (M1)\n\nTheir probabilities are \\((0.841344\\ldots)(0.941)\\) and \\((0.158655\\ldots)(0.217221\\ldots)\\). (A1)(A1)\n\nAdd the two route probabilities. (A1)\n\\[\nP(\\text{on time})=0.826168\\ldots\\approx\\boxed{0.826}. \\qquad \\text{A1 N2}\n\\]\n\n[5 marks]\n\n(e) Model the number of on-time arrivals as a binomial variable over \\(183\\) school days. (M1)\n\\[\nE(X)=183(0.826168\\ldots)=151.188\\ldots\\approx\\boxed{151}. \\qquad \\text{A1 N2}\n\\]\n\n[2 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\n(a)–(c) §14.3 The normal distribution · p. 603\n(d) §8.4 Probability tree diagrams · p. 381\n(e) §14.2 The binomial distribution · p. 592",
    "teacherNote": ""
