    "question": "The functions \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) are defined for \\(x\\in \\mathbb{R}\\) by \\(f(x)=x-2\\) and \\(g(x)=ax+b\\), where \\(a, b\\in \\mathbb{R}\\).\n\nGiven that \\((f\\circ g)(2)=-3\\) and \\((g\\circ f)(1)=5\\), find the value of \\(a\\) and the value of \\(b\\). [6]",
    "hint": "Which features or conditions of the function can be translated into equations? How do they determine the requested graph or function property?",
    "answer": "\\((f\\circ g)(x)=ax+b-2\\) (M1) \\((f\\circ g)(2)=-3\\Rightarrow 2a+b-2=-3 (2a+b=-1)\\) A1 \\((g\\circ f)(x)=a(x-2)+b\\) (M1) \\((g\\circ f)(1)=5\\Rightarrow -a+b=5\\) A1 a valid attempt to solve their two linear equations for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) M1 so \\(a=-2\\) and \\(b=3\\) A1 [6 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §2.2 Functional notation · p. 69",
    "teacherNote": ""
