    "imageAlt": "Circle with centre O, chord AB, point P on the circumference and central angle theta.",
    "question": "The following diagram shows a circle with centre \\(O\\) and radius \\(3\\).\n\n![Circle with centre O, chord AB, point P on the circumference and central angle theta.](images/QB000348_question_01_source.png)\n\nPoints \\(A\\), \\(P\\) and \\(B\\) lie on the circumference of the circle.\n\nChord \\([AB]\\) has length \\(L\\) and \\(A\\hat{O}B=\\theta\\) radians.\n\n(a) Show that arc \\(APB\\) has length \\(6\\pi -3\\theta\\). [2]\n\n(b) Show that \\(L=\\sqrt{18-18 \\cos \\theta }\\). [2]\n\n(c) Arc \\(APB\\) is twice the length of chord \\([AB]\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(\\theta\\). [3]",
    "hint": "Which features or conditions of the function can be translated into equations? How do they determine the requested graph or function property?",
    "answer": "(a) uses the arc length formula (M1) arc length is \\(3(2\\pi -\\theta )\\) A1 so arc \\(APB\\) has length \\(6\\pi -3\\theta\\) AG [2 marks]\n\nTherefore, the required result is established.\n\n(b) uses the cosine rule (M1) \\({L}^{2}={3}^{2}+{3}^{2}-2(3)(3) \\cos \\theta\\) A1 so \\(L=\\sqrt{18-18 \\cos \\theta }\\) AG [2 marks]\n\nTherefore, the required result is established.\n\n(c) \\(6\\pi -3\\theta =2\\sqrt{18-18 \\cos \\theta }\\) A1 attempts to solve for \\(\\theta\\) (M1) \\(\\theta =2.49\\) A1 [3 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §2.2 Functional notation · p. 69",
    "teacherNote": ""
