    "question": "The quadratic equation \\((k-1){x}^{2}+2x+(2k-3)=0\\), where \\(k\\in \\mathbb{R}\\), has real distinct roots.\n\nFind the range of possible values for \\(k\\). [5]",
    "hint": "Which features or conditions of the function can be translated into equations? How do they determine the requested graph or function property?",
    "answer": "attempts to find an expression for the discriminant, \\(Δ\\), in terms of \\(k\\) (M1) \\(Δ=4-4(k-1)(2k-3) (=-8{k}^{2}+20k-8)\\) (A1) Note: Award M1A1 for finding \\(x=\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-4(k-1)(2k-3)}}{2(k-1)}\\). attempts to solve \\(Δ>0\\) for \\(k\\) (M1) Note: Award M1 for attempting to solve \\(Δ=0\\) for \\(k\\). \\(\\frac{1}{2}<k<2\\) A1A1 Note: Award A1 for obtaining critical values \\(k=\\frac{1}{2}, 2\\) and A1 for correct inequality signs. [5 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §3.4 Graphing quadratic functions · p. 142",
