    "question": "A six-sided biased die is weighted in such a way that the probability of obtaining a “six” is \\(\\frac{7}{10}\\).\n\n(a) The die is tossed five times.\n\nFind the probability of obtaining at most three “sixes”. [3]\n\n(b) The die is tossed five times.\n\nFind the probability of obtaining the third “six” on the fifth toss. [3]",
    "hint": "Which probability or statistical model matches the information given? Which parameters or conditions must be identified before calculating?",
    "answer": "(a) recognition of binomial (M1) X ~ B(5, 0.7) attempt to find P (X ≤ 3) M1 = 0.472 (= 0.47178) A1 [3 marks]\n\n(b) recognition of 2 sixes in 4 tosses (M1)\n\nP (3rd six on the 5th toss) \\(=[(\\begin{matrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{matrix})\\times {(0.7)}^{2}\\times {(0.3)}^{2}]\\times 0.7(=0.2646\\times 0.7)\\) A1\n\n= 0.185 (= 0.18522) A1 [3 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §8.1 Theoretical and experimental probability · p. 354",
    "teacherNote": ""
