    "question": "Let \\({f}^{\\prime}(x)=\\frac{8x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\). Given that \\(f(0)=5\\), find \\(f(x)\\). [5]",
    "hint": "Which derivative or integral represents the requested quantity? How do the given conditions determine any constants, limits, or turning points?",
    "answer": "attempt to integrate (M1)\n\n\\(u=2{x}^{2}+1\\Rightarrow \\frac{du}{dx}=4x\\)\n\n\\(\\int \\frac{8x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}dx=\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{u}}du\\) (A1)\n\n\\(=4\\sqrt{u}(+C)\\) A1\n\n correct substitution into their integrated function (must have C) (M1)\n\n\\(5=4+C\\Rightarrow C=1\\)\n\n\\(f(x)=4\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}+1\\) A1\n\n[5 marks]",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §5.2 The derivative function · p. 222",
    "teacherNote": ""
