    "question": "The derivative of a function \\(f\\) is given by \\(f'(x)=3\\sqrt{x}\\).\n\nGiven that \\(f(1)=3\\), find the value of \\(f(4)\\). [6]",
    "hint": "Which derivative or integral represents the requested quantity? How do the given conditions determine any constants, limits, or turning points?",
    "answer": "\\(f(x)=\\int 3\\sqrt{x} dx\\) (A1) attempts to integrate (M1) \\(f(x)=2{x}^{\\frac{3}{2}}+C (=2x\\sqrt{x}+C)\\) A1 uses \\(f(1)=3\\) to obtain \\(3=2{(1)}^{\\frac{3}{2}}+C\\) and so \\(C=1\\) M1 substitutes \\(x=4\\) into their expression for \\(f(x)\\) (M1) so \\(f(4)=17\\) A1",
    "whereToLearn": "Mathematics: Analysis and Approaches Standard Level Course Companion · First edition, 2019\n\nWhole question §5.3 Differentiation rules · p. 234",
    "teacherNote": ""
