    "question": "The following diagram shows the graph of \\(y=4-{x}^{2}\\), \\(0\\leq x\\leq 2\\) and rectangle \\(ORST\\). The rectangle has a vertex at the origin \\(O\\), a vertex on the \\(y\\)-axis at the point \\(R(0, y)\\), a vertex on the \\(x\\)-axis at the point \\(T(x, 0)\\) and a vertex at point \\(S(x, y)\\) on the graph.\n\n![Quarter-circle graph with point S, intercepts R and T and a shaded rectangle.](images/QB000390_question_01_source.png)\n\nLet \\(P\\) represent the perimeter of rectangle \\(ORST\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(P=-2{x}^{2}+2x+8\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find the dimensions of rectangle \\(ORST\\) that has maximum perimeter and determine the value of the maximum perimeter. [6]\n\nLet \\(A\\) represent the area of rectangle \\(ORST\\).\n\n(c) Find an expression for \\(A\\) in terms of \\(x\\). [2]\n\n(d) Find the dimensions of rectangle \\(ORST\\) that has maximum area. [5]\n\n(e) Determine the maximum area of rectangle \\(ORST\\). [1]",
    "hint": "Which derivative or integral represents the requested quantity? How do the given conditions determine any constants, limits, or turning points?",
    "answer": "(a) \\(P=2x+2y\\) (A1) \\(=2x+2(4-{x}^{2})\\) A1 so \\(P=-2{x}^{2}+2x+8\\) AG [2 marks]\n\nTherefore, the required result is established.\n\n(b) uses the axis of symmetry of a quadratic (M1) \\(x=-\\frac{2}{2(-2)}\\) \\(x=\\frac{1}{2}\\) A1 substitutes their value of \\(x\\) into \\(y=4-{x}^{2}\\) (M1) \\(y=4-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\) \\(y=\\frac{15}{4}\\) A1 so the dimensions of rectangle \\(ORST\\) of maximum perimeter are \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) by \\(\\frac{15}{4}\\) EITHER substitutes their value of \\(x\\) into \\(P=-2{x}^{2}+2x+8\\) (M1) \\(P=-2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{2})+8\\) OR substitutes their values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) into \\(P=2x+2y\\) (M1) \\(P=2(\\frac{1}{2})+2(\\frac{15}{4})\\) \\(P=\\frac{17}{2}\\) A1 so the maximum perimeter is \\(\\frac{17}{2}\\)\n\n(c) substitutes \\(y=4-{x}^{2}\\) into \\(A=xy\\) (M1) \\(A=x(4-{x}^{2}) (=4x-{x}^{3})\\) A1 [2 marks]\n\n(d) \\(\\frac{dA}{dx}=4-3{x}^{2}\\) A1 attempts to solve their \\(\\frac{dA}{dx}=0\\) for \\(x\\) (M1) \\(4-3{x}^{2}=0\\) \\(\\Rightarrow x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}(=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}) (x>0)\\) A1 substitutes their (positive) value of \\(x\\) into \\(y=4-{x}^{2}\\) (M1) \\(y=4-{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\) \\(y=\\frac{8}{3}\\) A1 [5 marks]\n\n(e) \\(A=\\frac{16}{3\\sqrt{3}} (=\\frac{16\\sqrt{3}}{9})\\) A1 [1 mark]",
